WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.440
OK, so starting
from your roadmap,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.920
last Monday we started talking
about more modern approaches

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.000
to learning based control.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.360
Specifically, we looked
at imitation learning

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.900
as our specific paradigm of
interest where loosely speaking,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.680
we pitched imitation learning
as this idea of wanting

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.560
to learn from demonstrations.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.720
And so we saw different types of
ways of doing this, specifically

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.640
behavioral cloning and inverse
reinforcement learning, where

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.400
the idea there is that we want
to directly learn a policy that

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.080
represents or mimics
and approximates

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.572
the behavior of our expert.

00:00:41.572 --> 00:00:43.280
Or do we want to learn
the objective that

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.720
is, the underlying
reward function

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.560
that the expert is optimizing?

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.640
However, we also recognized how
imitation learning techniques

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.280
at their core are essentially
capped in terms of performance

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.720
by the performance of the
expert demonstrations.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.870
And so for today and for
the rest of this class,

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.628
we move towards
reinforcement learning,

00:01:03.628 --> 00:01:05.670
which, on the other hand,
is this idea of wanting

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.950
to learn from interactions.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.550
As a basic paradigm to learn
through trial and error

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:17.790
and discover solutions for
optimal control problems.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.090
So today and for the
next two classes,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.850
we'll be looking at model
free reinforcement learning,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.190
and then we'll be looking
at model-based reinforcement

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.630
learning.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.310
And specifically,
for today, we'll

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.750
start with a brief
recap of reinforcement

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.013
learning problem setting.

00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:35.430
And we'll dive a
little bit deeper

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.930
into what I'm referring
to here as exact methods.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.810
So if you recall in our
lectures on dynamic programming,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.030
we discussed value iteration
and policy iteration

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.950
as ways of solving Markov
decision processes.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.550
And so we'll look
a little bit more

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.390
closely on those that
is methods that leverage

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.850
dynamic programming for
computing solutions.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:58.770
The reason why I refer
to them as exact methods

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.250
is because implicitly,
these methods, that

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.210
is value iteration
and policy iteration,

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.930
were leveraging the fact
that we knew the underlying

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.130
dynamics of our system,
which is something

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.050
that on the other
hand, we assume

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.350
we won't have throughout
reinforcement learning.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.450
And this is where learning from
interaction will come in place.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.250
And so from that, we'll
then leverage and build

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.570
upon those techniques to
introduce two, I would say,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.850
really fundamental
kind of approaches

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.970
for model free
learning, that is model

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.050
learning from interactions.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.090
Specifically, we'll look
at Monte Carlo learning

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.690
and temporal
difference learning.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.530
And then we will wrap
up the discussion

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.230
with some kind of
concluding remarks

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.490
and as a segue into the
next few lectures we'll

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.810
be diving more closely into
specific algorithms, both model

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:45.870
free and model based.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:52.330
And so starting from
our problem setting,

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:57.080
we introduced in the past
this idea of a Markov Decision

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.340
Process, which were
ultimately an MDP,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.000
is represented by a tuple
of elements here defined

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.840
by the calligraphic m.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.200
Loosely speaking, we have
an action and a state space

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.600
which represent the possible
set of states and actions

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.780
our agent could either
be in or could enact.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.280
We have a transition
function, which

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.420
is represented in
the RL literature,

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.840
usually as a distribution
over next states given

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.180
states and controls.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.520
We have a reward function
which maps states and controls

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.320
to a scalar measure
of success, and we

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.660
have a discount factor, which is
just a scalar between 0 and 1.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.680
We discussed how this is
both useful from an intuitive

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.720
standpoint, it
kind of trades off

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.300
long term reward with
respect to short term reward,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.800
but also as a
mathematical object

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.880
that enables an infinite
sum to become tractable.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.060
Where does this infinite
sum come in place?

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.000
It comes in place
from the overall goal

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.830
that we want to maximize in when
we talk about Markov decision

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:54.490
processes.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:57.150
That is, we want to find the
optimal policy, pi star, that

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.230
maximizes the expected
cumulative discounted

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.570
reward over time.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.490
And to solve these
problems, last time,

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.150
we introduced the concepts
of value functions

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.990
and Bellman equations.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.510
Specifically, we introduced the
state value function represented

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:20.029
as v as essentially an element
that encodes the expected reward

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:23.710
that we can expect to have
starting from some state, xt,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.270
and then following
a given policy, pi.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:31.710
So the value function is an
object that depends on policy.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.190
And we saw how these
value functions

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.590
satisfy some fixed
point equations, namely

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.050
the Bellman equations.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.030
And so we discussed the Bellman
optimality equation, which

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.070
relates with the
optimal value function,

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:48.230
v star, which essentially
says that the optimal value

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.430
function, v star,
at some state xt,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:54.180
is essentially equal to the
maximization over controls,

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.940
u, ut, of the sum of
the instantaneous reward

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:03.020
plus the expected value
at the next state,

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:06.380
xt plus 1, where the expectation
is essentially averaging out

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.420
over possible next states.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.360
We saw how this is a property
of the optimal value function,

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.100
but we discussed this
how a similar fixed point

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.540
equation holds also
for any policy, pi,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.940
where very similarly, this
fixed point equation, that

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.940
is the Bellman
expectation equations

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.400
is defined as we introduce
also a couple of weeks back.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.560
We did a very similar
discussion for the Q function,

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.700
that is, essentially
a measure to represent

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:41.660
our expected future reward
starting from some state, xt,

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:45.260
taking an action, ut, and then
following a given policy, pi.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.820
Again, very similarly, here we
have some fixed point equations

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:52.520
which these concept
satisfy, which are namely

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.920
the Bellman optimality
equation and expectation

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.920
equation for the Q
function, respectively.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.740
Why did we introduce
the Q function?

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.900
We introduced it because
from the control,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.160
from the perspective of
developing an optimal policy,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.840
this is a pretty
useful in practice

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.920
because it allows us to define a
policy implicitly through the Q

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:19.920
function as the argmax
over u of the Q function

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.560
defined on some state, x.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.640
And so very
practically, at least

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.340
from the perspective
of tabular methods,

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.140
that is the one we'll
be looking at today,

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.920
so where we don't discuss
function approximation,

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.860
I think it's useful to
visualize value functions,

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.280
both state value functions and Q
functions as just lookup tables.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.640
So the value function, v of
x, you can namely visualize it

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:50.550
as the value for any specific
state x1 to xn, assuming we

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.390
have n possible states.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.910
And very similarly
for the Q function

00:06:55.910 --> 00:07:00.790
this would then be a matrix
representing possible Q values

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.170
for all states and all controls.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.990
OK, so we said how these
were essentially interesting

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:15.270
properties that derived from
this infinite horizon Markov

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.350
decision process
problem setting.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:21.110
And we then operationalize
these concepts

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.950
into practical
algorithms specifically

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.390
through, as we
were saying before,

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:28.870
value iteration and
policy iteration.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:30.990
And in these algorithms,
they essentially

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:37.670
boil down, if you remember, as
mechanisms where we iteratively

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:41.710
enforce these fixed points
equations until convergence.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:44.710
So in value iteration,
we recursively

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.660
enforce the Bellman
optimality equation

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.780
until we converge to the
optimal value function.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.340
And for policy iteration,
we alternate it

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.015
between step, which we call
the policy evaluation step.

00:07:57.015 --> 00:07:59.140
That is, the application
of the Bellman expectation

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.020
equation that we are
trying to estimate

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.060
the value of a given policy.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.280
And then we had the
policy improvement step

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.460
which turned out to be a greedy
policy improvement, in our case.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:12.300
So long story short, we have
seen all of these objects

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:12.860
before.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:16.180
And in the past, we derived
actionable algorithms

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:21.260
to solve Markov decision
processes by basically enforcing

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:22.600
these Bellman equations.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.380
But in order to
do this, we always

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.800
leveraged the knowledge
of the dynamics function.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.580
So looking a little bit more
closely at policy iteration,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.020
because this will be a concept
we'll be building on today,

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:42.020
if you recall, policy
iteration is essentially

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:46.490
an iterative algorithm where we
are given an initial policy, pi.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:49.610
And for every iteration,
k, that ranges from 1

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.410
until some convergence or
termination criteria is met.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.410
We're basically alternating
between these two processes

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:59.190
which visually we could
visualize here on this slide.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:02.490
We have a given initial
guess for our value function

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.210
and a given initial policy.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.890
And we alternate between
the policy evaluation

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:09.410
step, which basically
evaluates the value

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:11.210
function for that policy.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.550
And then we have a
policy improvement step,

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:17.690
which, on the other hand,
uses the most recent estimate

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:21.150
of the value function to derive
a better policy from that.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:27.090
How did these two
processes look in practice?

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:29.850
For the policy evaluation
step, we recursively

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:34.010
enforced the Bellman expectation
equation as we defined before.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.750
And for the policy
improvement step,

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:38.470
we were basically taking
the argmax over u.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:45.335
Yeah, so note again that in
both of these formulations,

00:09:45.335 --> 00:09:46.710
we're leveraging
the fact that we

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.850
have the knowledge of
the dynamics function.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.870
So more concretely,
let us look at how

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:58.950
this looks from
an implementation

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.330
perspective in practice.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.670
Let's consider this
gridworld example

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:08.550
where we have this basically
16 dimensional world where

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.710
we could be in either
of these grid cells.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:16.010
We have 14 non-terminal states,
that is the white cells.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:17.750
And we have two
terminal states or goal

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.670
states that we want to reach.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:21.230
So loosely speaking,
the goal here

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:25.250
is will be spawned in
any of these cells.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.150
And the goal is to get to
the goal terminal cells

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:31.830
as fast as possible.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:34.590
How do we encode this objective
of getting to the goal point

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:35.950
as fast as possible?

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:39.690
An idea could be to just
fix the reward to be minus 1

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:46.682
or any negative number until
we reach the terminal cell.

00:10:46.682 --> 00:10:48.140
In this case, we're
assuming we can

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.060
take these four possible
controls so the four

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.480
cardinal directions.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.500
And if we take some
of our controls

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:56.797
that would lead us
out of this grid cell,

00:10:56.797 --> 00:10:57.880
we just stay where we are.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:00.220
We're doing this assumption.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:01.800
And for simplicity
of calculation,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.580
we're assuming that we have
an undiscounted MDP where

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:05.680
gamma is equal to 1.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:12.620
So, in practice, let us take
a first step in the policy

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:13.920
iteration algorithm.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.038
So we say that we have
a given initial policy.

00:11:17.038 --> 00:11:18.580
And for now, for
simplicity, since we

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.720
have potentially no clue
about what a good policy is,

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.183
we just assume that our
policy is a random policy.

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:26.100
So in all states, we
just take a random action

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.860
with equal probability.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.740
Again, we have
potentially no clue

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.600
of how to initialize
the value function.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:36.660
So we might just
initialize it as all zeros.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:39.580
So here on the
left column you're

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.410
basically seeing
different iterations

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:46.330
of the policy evaluation step.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:50.730
So we are enforcing the Bellman
expectation equations at k

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:56.050
equals 1, k equals
2, until we converge.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:12:00.210
So concretely, say we are
at this grid cell over here.

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:05.970
So the coordinate 2,
2 what we're doing

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:10.450
is that we are
updating the value

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:20.010
at that specific state as the
current estimate of our value

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:22.690
function.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:32.570
So the reward plus the expected
future values in the next states

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:34.070
that we would expect to be in.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:37.810
So more formally, we have
this negative 1, which is just

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.640
the reward of taking one step.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.460
And then we have the expectation
of all possible next states,

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:47.040
which in our case, are equally
distributed around the four

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.120
possible neighboring states.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:13:03.020
So we'll be looking at xt plus
1 in 1, 2 to 1, 3, 2 and 2, 3.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.500
So we are looking at wanting
to update this cell over here.

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:15.880
So the reward of taking
any move is minus 1.

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:21.200
And then we have to take the
expected value of the next state

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:26.600
that we land in, which is in our
case one of these four states.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:30.360
And when we take the expectation
under our transition dynamics,

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:31.800
there is the
probability of being

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:36.160
xt plus 1 given xt
and the action that we

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:42.740
would take in xt times the
previous estimate of the value

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:43.240
function.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:48.880
So this is v1, if you wish, and
this would be v0 in that state.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.360
So here, nothing too fancy.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:58.180
I just basically formalized
what we are writing the Bellman

00:13:58.180 --> 00:13:59.620
expectation equation.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:00.380
Yes.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.900
Is it kind of just
convention that k

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:06.740
equals 0 you have all zeros
rather than just initializing

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:11.260
with the value or rather with
the reward for each state?

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:12.360
That's just a choice.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.380
So if you have knowledge
in general, knowledge

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:18.440
in your problem that you wish
to encode as a starting point,

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.460
then it's definitely a good idea
to do so because it just speeds

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:22.772
up the convergence.

00:14:22.772 --> 00:14:25.480
But in this case, we just assume
we have no knowledge whatsoever.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:27.820
And we start from all zeros.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.980
So in this case, well, we
know that our previous guess

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:31.800
was all zeros.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.260
So this is always 0.

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:37.450
In theory, we would know that
this is equally distributed

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:38.830
across our four steps.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:41.230
So this would be always 0.25.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:44.130
But, in general, since we have
a 0 term here, this cancels out.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.770
And so for all the grid
cells in k equals 1,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:50.850
we would actually get minus 1.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.290
And you can then imagine that
if we repeat this process

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:57.290
across iterations, we basically
get an evolving surface

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:58.750
of our value function.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:04.050
And if we do this
until convergence,

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:07.770
we would then get something
that looks like this.

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:10.130
So at this point in our
algorithm, essentially,

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:13.010
we have taken, again,
visualizing it here

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.850
in our sketch, we
basically took one step

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:17.550
in the policy evaluation step.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.130
So we concluded a
full policy evaluation

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:23.090
step where given a policy,
we estimated the value

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:24.070
for that policy.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:34.120
What is the next step then
here according to the policy

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:37.040
iteration algorithm?

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:37.880
Yes.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.080
We want to use those
expected values

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:43.420
to derive a different policy.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:44.300
Yeah, exactly.

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:47.160
So we want to then
take basically

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:49.800
do this step, which is the
policy improvement step.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.340
How did we define it so far?

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:58.600
We basically take this argmax
over this quantity over here

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:05.080
which essentially means that the
theta we converge to this value

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:08.320
function over here the policy
that maximizes this value

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:12.020
function, since the
reward is always minus 1,

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:16.000
no matter what we do, the
policy will basically converge

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:22.120
to taking the action, u,
which leads to the best,

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:25.680
the state with the best
value function value.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:28.000
So from here, we
could immediately even

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.380
visually see that in
each of these grid cells,

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.390
we're basically
the policy that we

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.670
get from that policy
improvement step essentially

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:38.150
would be pointing towards the
state with the neighboring state

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:42.550
with the best value
function, which

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:44.050
is exactly what we get here.

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:47.150
So this would be the
visualization of the policy

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:48.950
that we get out of
the policy improvement

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:52.730
step from this value function,
which we estimated before.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:04.750
Now, of course, if
you, in general, we

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:07.589
run a full policy evaluation
step until convergence

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:11.270
in this case, but you can always
hypothesize doing the policy

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.670
improvement step at intermediate
steps in our policy evaluation

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:15.450
iterations.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.650
In this case, since this
is a very simple problem,

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:20.910
you would actually
get the optimal policy

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.450
even if you stopped
before convergence.

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:26.089
But this is not guaranteed
to be the case, in general.

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:32.370
For example, here if we stopped
immediately and did the policy

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:33.890
improvement step
here, we would get

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.390
a policy that is still slightly
better from the original one

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:39.890
because we do get some
nice behavior here

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:43.870
where we're not random anymore,
but still not there yet.

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:44.370
Yes.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:51.890
Is not having-- is a stationary
policy enough of a signal

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:53.098
to say that you've converged?

00:17:53.098 --> 00:17:54.973
Or is it possible that
between iterations you

00:17:54.973 --> 00:17:56.270
could have a policy not change?

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.450
But if you continued
and the value function

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:00.650
continues changing then
you might update the policy

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:01.810
later on?

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:03.650
Yeah, so in general,
I guess the question

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:05.570
is, how do you define
termination criterion

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:06.450
for this case?

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:09.450
So, in general, for these
tabular settings where

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:13.010
we can actually enumerate all
the possible value functions,

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:16.290
so values for each states
and each state action pairs.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:18.850
This is proven to converge.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:20.010
So there is no--

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:23.130
if you run this long enough,
you will be converging.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.010
In general, there are different
types of termination criteria

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:27.070
you could come up with.

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:30.020
Some could be like delta based.

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:32.840
So depending on how much it
changes across iterations

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:37.220
and there is no real one
best rule for doing that,

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:38.867
if makes sense.

00:18:38.867 --> 00:18:40.700
So to answer to your
question, in this case,

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:42.117
as long as you run
it long enough,

00:18:42.117 --> 00:18:44.520
you will be converging
to the optimal value

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:45.820
function and policy.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.760
And so you will notice also
running through this example

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:00.610
that all of these
formulations, in this case,

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:01.860
we looked at policy iteration.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:04.480
But the same thing holds
for value iteration.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:07.180
All of these formulations
require a model of the MDP.

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:08.720
So we use the
transition dynamics

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:13.200
to update our quantities.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:15.460
In general, to
solve unknown MDPs,

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:20.440
that is MDPs where we don't
have access to the ground truth

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:23.040
transition dynamics,
we'll use interactions

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:26.430
with the environment to
replace this knowledge.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:31.950
And so more generally
speaking, the main limitations

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:36.030
of these exact methods, such
as policy and value iterations

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:37.830
are mainly two.

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:40.470
The first one is that the
update equations require access

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:44.630
to the dynamics, and we'll use
sampling-based approximations

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:49.270
to basically solve and
address this challenge.

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.030
And we'll see this more shortly.

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:54.590
And the other one is
that iteration over

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:57.870
and storage of all of
the states and actions

00:19:57.870 --> 00:19:59.790
and the values for
the states and actions

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:03.710
can require an extreme kind
of a large memory effort.

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:08.230
And also it could just lead to
very long far away convergence

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:09.430
for these algorithms.

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:13.270
And so whenever we have a
very large state action space

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.950
we'll see how
function approximation

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:16.910
could come in handy.

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:19.770
Today we'll be mostly looking at
sampling based approximations.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:22.070
But starting from
next week, we'll

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:24.010
also be looking at
function approximation.

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:31.050
Any questions here
before we get started

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:34.110
with the first algorithms?

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:43.130
So let's start from
Monte Carlo learning.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:47.810
And the idea of Monte Carlo
is actually extremely simple.

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:57.210
So we are saying that we
are interested in estimating

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:01.930
these values for value
functions in general.

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:06.030
And we want to do
this using samples.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.630
What do we mean with
samples in our case?

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:12.290
So we have an environment which
we assume we can interact with.

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:15.390
And interactions, essentially,
means collecting rollouts.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:19.050
So we will be releasing our
policy in the environment

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:22.030
and collecting the rollouts that
we get from the environment.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:26.840
So this means collecting a
sequence of states and controls

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:32.720
over time until we reach some
termination criteria here.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:37.360
Now, in principle, what we are
interested in when we talk about

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:41.720
in this case, for example,
the value of state x1

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:47.440
would be to basically
approximate the expected future

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:53.240
rewards that we would expect to
have if we were able to simulate

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:54.980
infinitely many scenarios.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:58.360
I'm interested in the ground
truth expectation of this value

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:00.420
function.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.640
To do this, so far,
we've leveraged knowledge

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:05.757
of the transition dynamics.

00:22:05.757 --> 00:22:07.340
But, in general,
this is not possible.

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:09.640
So we want to do this
through sampling.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.680
And this means that in
Monte Carlo, what we do

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:19.200
is that we basically want to
approximate this expected value

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.630
function or expected future
reward through the return.

00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:24.190
The return for us,
let's remember,

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:27.490
that it's just the-- or at
least the return of a trajectory

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:31.790
in this case, at time t, is
just the sum of all the rewards

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:34.650
that we observed
from time t onwards.

00:22:43.270 --> 00:22:46.150
Again, simply put, the
return starting from x1

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:49.110
would just be having
our trajectory

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:52.110
evaluating all of the
rewards that we got over time

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:53.250
and summing them up.

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:01.750
And the idea is that if we were
to repeat this multiple times,

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.290
so we don't have
only one trajectory,

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.990
but we have multiple,
we would be essentially

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:10.790
able to approximate this ground
truth expectation that we're

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:14.690
interested in through the mean,
the empirical mean, essentially.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:21.800
Does this make sense?

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:35.100
And so because of this,
essentially, Monte Carlo methods

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:39.360
are methods that directly learn
from episodes of experience.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:40.860
And it is model free.

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:42.900
So there is no knowledge
of the transitions

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:44.040
that we're using here.

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:47.660
We're just using interactions
or samples from our environment

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:49.160
to approximate these values.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:55.480
One caveat, of course, which
is also a pretty important one,

00:23:55.480 --> 00:24:00.020
is that because of how we define
the return, which is literally

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:02.180
the sum of all the
rewards that we observe

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:07.220
throughout our trajectory,
Monte Carlo methods apply only

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:09.440
to episodic MDPs.

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:11.300
So there needs to
be a termination

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:14.580
of our episode at some point
simply because otherwise, we

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:19.720
would be continuing to summing
up rewards forever, essentially.

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:26.440
So how do we use
this in practice?

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:28.560
Kind of an immediate
thought there

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:32.080
would be to use Monte Carlo
methods for learning the state

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:36.040
value function, v. So
recall that we defined

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:40.640
the return as just the
discounted sum of future rewards

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:43.320
from given a trajectory.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.040
In principle, we are
interested in approximating

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.500
this expectation here, which
is just our value function.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:53.920
So, for us, the value function
is the expected future reward

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:58.200
that I expect to have starting
in xt following a policy, pi.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:00.960
And if you look at
this inner term here,

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:04.480
this is nothing else
than the return itself.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:11.400
So of the value function
is actually the expectation

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:15.060
of the return given state xt.

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:24.790
And so with Monte Carlo methods,
we essentially or specifically

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:27.390
with Monte Carlo
policy evaluation what

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.190
we would be doing is that
we use the empirical mean

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:34.870
to estimate this expectation
instead of computing

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:38.050
exactly this expectation through
the knowledge of the dynamics.

00:25:51.590 --> 00:25:57.790
In practice, you could have two
variants of Monte Carlo methods.

00:25:57.790 --> 00:26:02.030
The first is referred to as
first visit Monte Carlo, where

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:06.510
in order to evaluate the value
function at a given state,

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:09.450
we would be essentially
computing these means,

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:11.190
these mean returns.

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:16.980
Only the first time we visit a
specific state in an episode.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:21.100
So, in practice, once we
visit a specific state, x,

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:24.300
we would be incrementing the
counter of how many times

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:26.720
we visited that state x.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.300
We would be incrementing
the total return.

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:30.940
So that is all the
returns that we ever

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:34.560
saw when visiting that state
and then just taking the mean.

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:43.020
And, in general, the assuming
we have the more samples

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:44.980
of this trajectory
we would have.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:47.300
So with n going to
infinity, we would

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:49.080
be converging to the ground.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:50.640
Truth expectation.

00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:58.460
The other approach is
the every visit approach,

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:01.660
where now we account
explicitly for the situation

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:06.100
where maybe within our
rollout, for some reason,

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:09.360
we are maybe visiting
state x1 again.

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:13.150
So we are maybe
taking some detour,

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:15.410
and we end up in the
starting state again.

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:19.490
In the first visit, we would
basically be ignoring the return

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:21.350
from the second visit onwards.

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:23.010
In the every visit,
we just consider

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:29.450
these two as independent visits
and update the mean regardless.

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:32.090
Again, this is just a kind
of technicality when you look

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:34.690
at implementation, but the
overall idea is that we want

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:40.090
to approximate these expected
future rewards because we cannot

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:42.470
compute these
expectations exactly,

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:45.070
we approximate them
through an empirical mean.

00:27:52.103 --> 00:27:54.650
[INAUDIBLE] revisit the same
state over and over again to do

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:58.050
different trajectories,
wouldn't it be--

00:27:58.050 --> 00:27:59.910
I don't know if unfair
is the right word,

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.690
but to exclude that
from your database

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:06.050
with the first visit
mentality rather than doing

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:08.170
the every visit mentality?

00:28:08.170 --> 00:28:10.250
Yes, I think it's a
valid point, especially

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:13.670
if you have these maybe
degenerate cases where

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:17.888
the policy is visiting
the state over and over.

00:28:17.888 --> 00:28:19.430
I would say in
general, this probably

00:28:19.430 --> 00:28:24.950
boils down to you want to
have sufficient exploration.

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:28.230
So you want a policy that
basically explores your state

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:32.510
action space well
enough for these tools

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:36.390
to be efficient and
effective in practice.

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:38.090
But yes, so when you
have these cases,

00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:42.230
it is a valid discussion
to consider like,

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:45.730
should I go for an every visit
example or a first visit case?

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:51.130
Maybe in that case, the
first visit is what you want.

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:54.850
If you're saying this
will be used [INAUDIBLE],

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:57.550
site an example
like in practice.

00:28:57.550 --> 00:29:00.950
[INAUDIBLE] if you're not
able to run this and collect

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:04.150
this data on simulation based
on some model that you have,

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:05.990
it would be
difficult in practice

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:10.620
to set up some experiment
and collect all this data.

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:12.880
Yes, this is a good
question because in general,

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:15.780
so here, when we talk about
model free, we refer to the fact

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:19.820
that we're not leveraging a
known model of the dynamics

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:21.700
within our calculations.

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:26.460
Now, you could indirectly
use it by having interactions

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:28.420
with the simulator.

00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:33.580
But, in general, here this
is agnostic to any kind

00:29:33.580 --> 00:29:36.720
of environment and reality
we are interacting with.

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:38.940
So by model free
here, we just mean

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:43.040
that we are modifying all
of our update equations,

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:46.540
saying the policy
iteration case to avoid

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:51.780
using an analytical expression
of the transition dynamics.

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:55.740
So in practice, I would say
leveraging a simulator here

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:58.000
would be a concrete
example of using this.

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:02.220
So we have some simulator that
simulates our environment,

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:03.780
say a driving simulator.

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:07.690
And then we want to use these
tools to estimate a value

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:10.030
function within that
simulated world.

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:12.210
But we're doing this
without leveraging

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:14.890
the potentially extremely
complex model that

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:18.510
runs under the hood in the
simulation, if that makes sense.

00:30:21.570 --> 00:30:23.070
Yes, does it clarify?

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:28.930
Yeah, so bottom line is model
3 here just refers to the fact

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:31.070
that we're not using
the transition dynamics.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:42.310
So let's look at an example.

00:30:42.310 --> 00:30:46.250
So here consider the game of
blackjack, which we're probably

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:47.990
most of us are familiar with.

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:49.890
But in general,
here we are assuming

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:56.350
that we have a state where
we are dealt two cards.

00:30:56.350 --> 00:30:59.530
We're playing against a dealer
that also has two cards.

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:03.090
The state for us is that we
are considering the current sum

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:08.000
of the two cards that we have in
hand that ranges from 12 to 21.

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:12.520
We're also specifically
recognizing

00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:16.420
if the dealer's showing card.

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:19.440
So we have visibility of one
of the two cards of the dealer,

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:21.200
so from ace to 10.

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:25.280
And we are specifically having
a flag if we have a usable ace

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:25.780
or not.

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:29.665
So we have an ace
in hand or not?

00:31:29.665 --> 00:31:31.040
In this case, of
course, the goal

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:38.080
is to beat some of the dealer
essentially having the hand

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:41.360
that reaches closer to 21.

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:44.000
The way we do this is by--

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:45.620
so we have two possible actions.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:47.360
So at any point
in time, either we

00:31:47.360 --> 00:31:51.480
stand so we stop receiving
cards and the game terminates

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:54.200
or we hit, that is, we
ask for another card

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:57.040
without replacement.

00:31:57.040 --> 00:32:00.880
In this case, the reward for
us is that when we stand.

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:04.460
So where the game terminates,
we get a plus 1 reward

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:08.300
if some of the cards essentially
beats one of the dealer,

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:09.160
we have 0.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:12.340
If we have a tie, and we
have a negative reward

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:17.480
if we lose against the
dealer for the hit,

00:32:17.480 --> 00:32:20.360
when we actually ask
for another card,

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:23.460
we get a negative
reward if we go above 21

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:27.000
where the game terminates
and we get 0 otherwise.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:34.100
So we, in general, we are
assuming that we automatically

00:32:34.100 --> 00:32:37.240
hit if our sum of
cards is below 12.

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:40.460
This is the only assumption
that we have on the transitions.

00:32:40.460 --> 00:32:43.160
So in this case, we want
to consider a given policy.

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:46.540
So we are given to us this
policy which basically stands

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:48.620
if the sum is greater
or equal than 20

00:32:48.620 --> 00:32:50.700
and always hits otherwise.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:52.980
And so the goal here
is let's now take

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:54.860
this environment
with these rules

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:58.620
and basically run policy
evaluation through Monte Carlo

00:32:58.620 --> 00:33:00.600
sampling in this environment.

00:33:03.410 --> 00:33:05.250
Is this setting clear?

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:06.970
Nice.

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:10.730
So we'll be blackjack
experts after this, I guess.

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:15.570
So what we're looking at here
is assume we are running policy

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:21.550
evaluation for 10,000
episodes or 500,000 episodes.

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:23.850
So again, here what
we're saying this

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:26.470
would be one episode
of interaction.

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:31.090
We're assuming we are playing
10,000 and 500,000 games with

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:32.770
the dealer.

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:36.250
And these three
dimensional landscapes

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:40.090
are essentially a visualization
of the value function.

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:42.610
So here, specifically,
we have two axes.

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:47.210
One axis is looking at the
dealer showing card that

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:49.210
ranges from an ace to a 10.

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:52.050
We have a player sum,
so the sum that we

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:54.170
have in hand from 12 to 21.

00:33:54.170 --> 00:33:59.050
And we have the surface
kind of differentiated

00:33:59.050 --> 00:34:01.040
between when we
have a usable ace

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:03.080
and when we don't
have a usable ace.

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:06.680
So this is just our state space
if you remember those three

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:08.960
bullet points.

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:13.120
So what we see is, first of all,
you see a difference between

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:17.120
the column that relates with the
10,000 episodes and the 500,000

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:19.960
episodes in terms of noise.

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:22.320
So these estimates
are definitely

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:28.320
more noisy than the ones
where we have more episodes.

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.080
Again, the reality
is that here we

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:32.120
are approximating
these expectations

00:34:32.120 --> 00:34:33.500
through empirical means.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:35.179
And so the more
episodes we have,

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:37.980
the more we are converging
to the ground truth solution.

00:34:41.719 --> 00:34:45.840
And to basically
maybe understand

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:48.840
to get your understanding here,
let us look at some questions

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:50.480
and have some questions for you.

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:54.800
So consider specifically
the diagrams on the right.

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:58.390
So why does the
estimated value function

00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:03.590
jump up for the last two
rows here in the back?

00:35:03.590 --> 00:35:04.510
Yeah?

00:35:04.510 --> 00:35:09.570
Because those are the only
thing [INAUDIBLE] 20 and 21,

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.950
we always hit until we
get there either most

00:35:11.950 --> 00:35:13.130
of the time we're in a bust.

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:14.910
But if we do get
there, we're probably

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:17.710
going to win [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:18.530
Yeah, exactly.

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:21.690
So we're playing a game with
our policy that is given to us,

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:24.170
where we only stand
if we are 20, 21.

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:29.830
And if we do, that's a pretty
good hand so we likely win.

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:31.350
So exactly correct.

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:35.430
And another question would be,
why does the value function

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:39.690
drop off for the whole last row
on the left on the other hand?

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:48.710
When the dealer has
an ace, so they're

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:50.910
likely to have a 10 to get one.

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:51.430
Exactly.

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:56.050
So also just having
an ace is a good hand.

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:58.030
So you can use this
in different ways.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:36:00.690
And so that's on average
is something where

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:03.890
the dealer is well positioned.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:07.930
So, in general, of course you
see how the bottom line here

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:12.170
is that in policy
evaluation, we basically

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:17.610
had no information whatsoever
on how the game works

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:19.970
or any dynamics of this game.

00:36:19.970 --> 00:36:22.550
We basically leveraged
simulation, if you wish,

00:36:22.550 --> 00:36:24.850
or interactions
with the environment

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:29.390
to estimate future
values of all the states.

00:36:29.390 --> 00:36:32.370
And what we get is
through this interaction,

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:37.290
a complete representation of the
value of every possible state

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:39.970
that we could find
ourselves in this game.

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:42.950
And so given this policy
that is given to us,

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:47.410
we have exactly
characterized its efficacy

00:36:47.410 --> 00:36:50.890
in all different states,
if that make sense.

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:54.090
And this value function
is, to some extent,

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:57.120
interpretable because we can
more or less understand it

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:58.560
and it's reasonable.

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:10.080
As a just note,
in general, we're

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:12.140
talking about in Monte Carlo.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:16.160
We're leveraging empirical means
to approximate expectations.

00:37:16.160 --> 00:37:21.840
Of course, these episodes will
be coming in incrementally.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:25.920
And so it doesn't make sense to
recompute the means from scratch

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:29.100
or store all the possible
rewards that we ever observed.

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:33.300
But, in practice, we often
compute the means incrementally.

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:36.040
And so we know that this is
a standard result that we

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:43.040
can compute the mean for
a given realization, x,

00:37:43.040 --> 00:37:47.520
incrementally through
this equation over here,

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:50.600
where we're essentially
updating the mean

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:54.150
as the previous
estimate plus basically

00:37:54.150 --> 00:38:00.550
a step in the direction
of the new observation.

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:03.710
So this is, in general,
for incremental means.

00:38:03.710 --> 00:38:07.030
Of course, for us, the mean that
we're interested in computing

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:09.790
is the expected return,
the mean return,

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:12.590
which for us, is our
estimate of the value.

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:16.310
And so we'll be
updating our value,

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:20.070
our estimate for the value
function as the previous guess

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:24.110
plus basically a step in the
direction of the new return

00:38:24.110 --> 00:38:25.170
that we have observed.

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:33.590
So this is the exact formulation
for the incremental mean.

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:38.070
In practice, we will often see
this expressed very similarly

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:43.590
to what you would see a gradient
descent step update rule where

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:47.150
we could have a varying
alpha, basically

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:49.230
a scalar that changes
how big of a step

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:51.370
we're taking in the
direction of the update.

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:04.470
Any questions on Monte Carlo
before we move to TD learning?

00:39:09.450 --> 00:39:13.170
So Monte Carlo is
one, I would say,

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:16.930
really fundamental strategy
for model free learning.

00:39:16.930 --> 00:39:19.450
TD learning is, I would
say, the other bigger player

00:39:19.450 --> 00:39:23.890
in when we talk about model
free learning techniques.

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:25.810
And specifically,
at a high level,

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:28.930
TD learning is a
combination of Monte Carlo

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.610
and dynamic
programming approaches.

00:39:31.610 --> 00:39:35.830
So like Monte Carlo, TD
learning is model free,

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:41.610
so there is no knowledge of the
transition dynamics in our MDP.

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:43.930
And TD learning also
learns from experience.

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:45.570
So we are replacing
this knowledge

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:48.030
through interactions
with the environment.

00:39:50.560 --> 00:39:52.600
On the other hand,
different kind

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:55.200
of differing from Monte
Carlo but similarly

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:58.320
to dynamic programming,
temporal difference

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:03.080
learning update estimates
of, for example, values

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:06.340
based in part on other
learned estimates.

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:10.920
Essentially, we can update
our guess towards a guess

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:15.560
without waiting for the final
outcome in our episodes.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:21.280
More formally, it is denoted
as temporal difference learning

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:24.280
and dynamic
programming bootstrap.

00:40:24.280 --> 00:40:30.580
So remember how in
dynamic programming,

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:32.600
we were essentially
saying that we

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:39.960
could have a recursive function
where the value function for us

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:44.900
would be at a state xt would be
equal to the immediate return,

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:50.870
a reward that we see at xt
plus a discounted future value

00:40:50.870 --> 00:40:54.810
at the next state, xt.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:41:00.390
Now, in TD learning, since
we cannot compute these

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:03.010
expectations exactly,
because in order to do this,

00:41:03.010 --> 00:41:06.030
we would have to have knowledge
of our transition dynamics.

00:41:06.030 --> 00:41:10.390
We use samples to
approximate this expectation.

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:13.710
So in Monte Carlo, we were
using the full trajectory

00:41:13.710 --> 00:41:17.550
to directly estimate the return.

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:22.670
In TD, we are only interested
in the one step update

00:41:22.670 --> 00:41:28.950
where we will be
updating the value of x1

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:34.670
basically in the direction
of the immediate reward

00:41:34.670 --> 00:41:41.010
that we see, R1 plus the
discounted value at x2,

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:43.110
in this case.

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:45.030
So we'll be using
the same property

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:49.260
that we can use our current
estimate for the value function

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:53.540
to basically approximate
all future rewards

00:41:53.540 --> 00:41:59.580
that we would have observed
going forward with the episode.

00:41:59.580 --> 00:42:02.900
So more concretely, let us look
at the specific definition.

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:06.700
In Monte Carlo, we
said that we want

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:11.420
to update our value estimate
towards the actual return, GT.

00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:14.620
Specifically, we
take a step alpha

00:42:14.620 --> 00:42:18.980
in the direction of the return.

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:20.740
In temporal difference
learning, we

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:24.780
update the value towards
the estimated return

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:27.740
where this estimate is
taken using basically

00:42:27.740 --> 00:42:29.980
dynamic programming
ideas, where we

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:32.420
take the immediate reward
plus the discounted

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:35.280
future estimate of our value.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.020
And so, specifically,
we take a step

00:42:40.020 --> 00:42:44.880
in the direction of this
target over here in red.

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:49.520
So, in general, the reward plus
the discounted future value

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:52.040
is referred to as the TD target.

00:42:52.040 --> 00:42:57.840
And the difference between
our guess and this TD target

00:42:57.840 --> 00:42:59.880
is known as the TD error.

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:05.920
So, in general, you see how
temporal difference methods

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:08.410
combine the sampling
of Monte Carlo

00:43:08.410 --> 00:43:10.160
because we're only
leveraging interactions

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:12.680
with the environment
to estimate our values

00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:14.980
with the bootstrapping
of dynamic programming

00:43:14.980 --> 00:43:17.500
because we're updating
a guess towards a guess.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:24.612
Yes.

00:43:24.612 --> 00:43:26.320
For the first one,
the GT, does that mean

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:29.880
you have to run a bunch of
rollouts at each update step

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:32.320
to figure out what the
actual return would be?

00:43:32.320 --> 00:43:36.240
So here this is independent
of how many rollouts you do.

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:38.000
So here we're
saying that we want

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:44.450
to approximate
the actual return.

00:43:44.450 --> 00:43:47.590
So that is the sum of all
future rewards through sampling.

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:50.470
Specifically, here we
could take one sample

00:43:50.470 --> 00:43:55.590
of the return, which just means
summing all the rewards that we

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:58.470
see over time.

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:02.630
Visually, we want to approximate
this ground truth expectation

00:44:02.630 --> 00:44:03.750
over here.

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:08.910
And we could do this by just
evaluating the empirical returns

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:12.750
that we observe over
time and taking a mean.

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:16.550
Now, GT is just the
definition of the return.

00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:19.590
And so for us, assuming
we have one sample,

00:44:19.590 --> 00:44:21.310
this would be a
one sample estimate

00:44:21.310 --> 00:44:23.950
of the actual expectation.

00:44:23.950 --> 00:44:26.630
In temporal difference
learning, instead of

00:44:26.630 --> 00:44:29.270
looking at the
final return here,

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:32.790
we actually look at
the one step estimate

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:35.190
of that return where
this estimate is just

00:44:35.190 --> 00:44:39.750
the instantaneous reward,
R1, plus the discounted

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:42.420
guess of the value here.

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:44.260
So, in other words,
our guess here

00:44:44.260 --> 00:44:46.060
is just a mechanism
to avoid having

00:44:46.060 --> 00:44:48.140
to run through
the entire episode

00:44:48.140 --> 00:44:51.140
but approximating what
this rest of the return

00:44:51.140 --> 00:44:55.460
would be at time
2, in this case.

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:56.600
Does this clarify?

00:45:04.012 --> 00:45:07.000
[INAUDIBLE]

00:45:14.020 --> 00:45:16.140
So, in general,
so the interaction

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:21.560
will always be
for full rollouts.

00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:24.060
So we'll be having
full interactions

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:25.780
with our environment.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:29.980
But then this is changing how
we update our value estimates.

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:33.020
In the Monte Carlo case, we
update our value estimates

00:45:33.020 --> 00:45:37.140
looking basically one episode
at a time the full episode.

00:45:37.140 --> 00:45:41.130
In TD learning, we can actually
update our value estimates

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:43.250
more often, because
this would basically

00:45:43.250 --> 00:45:47.030
be only one update,
if that makes sense,

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:49.730
because we only have one return.

00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:53.730
Here, we can update
our value function

00:45:53.730 --> 00:45:56.290
before the episode finishes,
potentially, because we just

00:45:56.290 --> 00:45:59.156
use our guess to do so.

00:45:59.156 --> 00:46:00.930
[INAUDIBLE]

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:02.430
Potentially, potentially.

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:13.890
Yeah, so looking at
differences more closely,

00:46:13.890 --> 00:46:16.490
we mentioned how Monte
Carlo learning essentially

00:46:16.490 --> 00:46:19.930
requires episodic MDPs because
we need to reach that terminal

00:46:19.930 --> 00:46:21.730
state at the end.

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:24.770
On the other hand, in TD
learning, because we are only

00:46:24.770 --> 00:46:28.850
using our own guess to
estimate the values,

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:33.330
TD learning can learn before
the final outcome, essentially.

00:46:33.330 --> 00:46:34.930
And so TD learning
can potentially

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:36.930
learn online after every step.

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.670
But Monte Carlo must wait
until the end of the episode

00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:41.250
to collect those returns.

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:48.530
And so, indeed, TD learning can
learn without the final outcome

00:46:48.530 --> 00:46:53.110
and so can learn from
potentially incomplete sequences

00:46:53.110 --> 00:46:56.910
and can potentially work in
continuing or non-terminating

00:46:56.910 --> 00:46:57.692
environments.

00:46:57.692 --> 00:46:59.150
But, on the other
hand, Monte Carlo

00:46:59.150 --> 00:47:00.970
requires this determination.

00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:12.930
Does this make sense?

00:47:17.670 --> 00:47:20.590
So I would say, really,
Monte Carlo learning and TD

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:25.150
learning are the fundamental
mechanism through which we

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:28.070
estimate quantities of interest.

00:47:28.070 --> 00:47:31.630
Most of the times, these are
values and value functions.

00:47:31.630 --> 00:47:36.260
And that these are really
the foundations over which

00:47:36.260 --> 00:47:38.660
all other kind of reinforcement
learning algorithms

00:47:38.660 --> 00:47:40.020
are built on.

00:47:40.020 --> 00:47:43.620
Either if these are estimated
are kind of value-based methods

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:45.660
or policy optimization
methods we will

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:47.900
see actor-critic, and so forth.

00:47:47.900 --> 00:47:51.780
All of these tools essentially
rely on some approximation

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:53.160
of value functions.

00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:55.880
And when we don't have a
knowledge of the transition,

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:58.580
then we have to
resort to interactions

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:02.380
with the environment and
leverage tools similar to Monte

00:48:02.380 --> 00:48:04.680
Carlo and TD learning to do so.

00:48:09.852 --> 00:48:11.000
[INAUDIBLE]

00:48:17.340 --> 00:48:20.380
How could that working out for
environments like-- the way I'm

00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:22.620
understanding is that
do not recursively

00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:25.500
go back from the final
state to understand

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:28.860
the value of that final state
and work backwards to, say,

00:48:28.860 --> 00:48:31.500
that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:36.872
I think I'm misunderstanding how
you would do that [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:36.872 --> 00:48:39.330
No, I think you have the right
intuition because basically,

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:43.290
what you're saying is, I get
that the point that we're

00:48:43.290 --> 00:48:45.970
updating our guests
towards our next guests

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:49.670
and how this could mechanically
work online at each step,

00:48:49.670 --> 00:48:51.430
even without relying
on the final step,

00:48:51.430 --> 00:48:53.870
but you're saying at
the end of the day,

00:48:53.870 --> 00:48:58.103
we need to ground some of our
estimates on an actual value.

00:48:58.103 --> 00:48:59.270
And I think this is correct.

00:48:59.270 --> 00:49:03.430
And this is exactly where the
discount factor comes into play

00:49:03.430 --> 00:49:09.150
because if you take the extreme
version where the final,

00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:12.450
we are at step
500,000 in the future,

00:49:12.450 --> 00:49:16.890
then here the future values will
essentially be very close to 0.

00:49:16.890 --> 00:49:20.770
And so we will have a way
to estimate these value

00:49:20.770 --> 00:49:23.110
functions that are not
exploding to infinity,

00:49:23.110 --> 00:49:24.130
if that makes sense.

00:49:30.610 --> 00:49:32.720
OK, so we're
basically saying we're

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:36.400
interested in estimating
values and value functions.

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:38.640
We introduced these
two ways of doing so,

00:49:38.640 --> 00:49:42.640
Monte Carlo and TD learning,
which are essentially

00:49:42.640 --> 00:49:44.880
estimators of a
quantity that we're

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:46.420
interested in approximating.

00:49:46.420 --> 00:49:50.680
That is the expected future
reward or the cost to go,

00:49:50.680 --> 00:49:52.480
the reward to go, in this case.

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:56.420
So let us look at properties
of these two estimators.

00:49:56.420 --> 00:49:59.720
So specifically, the
bias and variance

00:49:59.720 --> 00:50:01.640
of these two estimators.

00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:07.440
So let us take Monte
Carlo to start.

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:09.720
So we're interested
in estimating

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:13.760
the true value of our policy.

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:15.940
And we're saying that
in order to do so,

00:50:15.940 --> 00:50:17.880
we want to use the
return, which is just

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:20.040
the sum, the discounted
sum of all the rewards

00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:21.480
that we observed.

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:25.120
Now, of course, this return
is an unbiased estimate

00:50:25.120 --> 00:50:28.400
of the ground truth value.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:35.620
Again, using an extreme example,
if the expectation that we're

00:50:35.620 --> 00:50:38.380
actually interested in
would be the expectation

00:50:38.380 --> 00:50:40.940
over all possible
future evolutions

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:43.580
or episodes in our environment.

00:50:43.580 --> 00:50:48.320
And the return is just
a sample from this.

00:50:48.320 --> 00:50:52.740
If we take an infinite amount
of interactions and episodes

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:56.080
with our environment, we would
be converging to the ground

00:50:56.080 --> 00:50:58.220
truth expectation.

00:50:58.220 --> 00:51:00.900
So in this sense, this
estimator is actually

00:51:00.900 --> 00:51:04.380
an unbiased estimate.

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:09.100
In principle, the
true TD target that

00:51:09.100 --> 00:51:11.420
is for temporal
difference learning,

00:51:11.420 --> 00:51:16.100
is also an unbiased estimator
of the actual value.

00:51:16.100 --> 00:51:19.860
But this is only if we assume we
already know what the true value

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:21.220
function is.

00:51:21.220 --> 00:51:24.100
So here we're saying
we're using this value

00:51:24.100 --> 00:51:28.260
function at the next step
to basically approximate

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:30.850
the future reward.

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:33.310
And if the value
function is exact,

00:51:33.310 --> 00:51:36.370
then this would actually be
an exact characterization

00:51:36.370 --> 00:51:38.530
of the expected reward to go.

00:51:38.530 --> 00:51:41.570
But, in reality, we
won't be using the exact,

00:51:41.570 --> 00:51:43.790
the true value
function, basically,

00:51:43.790 --> 00:51:46.290
just for the purpose
of learning is trying

00:51:46.290 --> 00:51:47.910
to estimate the value function.

00:51:47.910 --> 00:51:49.950
And so we will actually
be using an estimate.

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:52.970
Kind of a v hat for our value.

00:51:52.970 --> 00:51:56.450
And so since this
estimate won't be exact,

00:51:56.450 --> 00:51:58.190
we're introducing some bias.

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:06.430
Does this make sense?

00:52:14.130 --> 00:52:17.050
OK, and how about variance?

00:52:17.050 --> 00:52:26.330
So, in general, the TD target is
a much lower variance estimator

00:52:26.330 --> 00:52:29.280
than the return.

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:31.440
And this is because
the return depends

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:35.300
on a full sequence of
random actions, transitions,

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:38.000
and the reward realizations.

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:41.160
On the other hand, the
TD error kind of only

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:46.080
depends on one kind of sample
of basically stochasticity

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:49.320
of a random action
transition and reward.

00:52:49.320 --> 00:52:52.520
So here we're
saying that in order

00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:54.960
to approximate the
future reward to go,

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:57.640
we are either approximating
through the return

00:52:57.640 --> 00:53:00.800
that we evaluate here
or we approximate it

00:53:00.800 --> 00:53:05.540
through the TD target.

00:53:05.540 --> 00:53:11.840
So basically, in this
case, r1 plus v x1.

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:19.280
So in the former case, in
order to compute the return GT,

00:53:19.280 --> 00:53:22.080
we're basically
sampling multiple times

00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:25.590
over the episode
where sampling means

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:29.270
we have potentially unknown
kind of stochastic transition

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:35.550
dynamics that we are
repeatedly sampling from.

00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:41.210
We have potentially
stochastic policy

00:53:41.210 --> 00:53:43.510
that we're potentially
sampling from.

00:53:43.510 --> 00:53:46.410
And the reward itself
might be stochastic reward.

00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:51.490
On the other hand, in the TD for
the TD error for the TD target,

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:55.110
we're doing the same
sampling but only once.

00:53:55.110 --> 00:54:00.510
And so in this sense, the return
as an estimate of the true value

00:54:00.510 --> 00:54:03.110
is actually a much
higher variance estimator

00:54:03.110 --> 00:54:07.310
because there are just
many more possible futures

00:54:07.310 --> 00:54:09.810
that we are kind of taking
into consideration here.

00:54:18.910 --> 00:54:23.650
So let's really drive
this message home.

00:54:23.650 --> 00:54:25.970
And I think it's useful
to visualize this

00:54:25.970 --> 00:54:29.570
through these sort of
tree-like diagrams.

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:33.590
And specifically, let us
look at Monte Carlo first.

00:54:33.590 --> 00:54:36.410
So we basically
introduced what is often

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:38.730
referred to as
Monte Carlo backup,

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:42.490
meaning the update equation that
we use for our value estimates

00:54:42.490 --> 00:54:45.050
according to Monte
Carlo learning.

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:49.450
And we defined this as updating
our guess of the value function

00:54:49.450 --> 00:54:53.770
by taking a step in the
direction of the return.

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:55.490
What does this mean visually?

00:54:55.490 --> 00:54:59.210
In this diagram,
white nodes here

00:54:59.210 --> 00:55:04.810
represent states and black
nodes represent actions.

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:07.830
And t represents
a terminal state.

00:55:07.830 --> 00:55:09.690
So that is where the
episode terminates.

00:55:09.690 --> 00:55:12.850
And so you see how
potentially in state xt

00:55:12.850 --> 00:55:15.410
we could be taking
two possible actions.

00:55:15.410 --> 00:55:17.610
And depending on the
action that we take,

00:55:17.610 --> 00:55:22.640
we could land on potentially
two different states.

00:55:22.640 --> 00:55:24.920
In a Monte Carlo
backup, this essentially

00:55:24.920 --> 00:55:30.120
means that in this possible
tree of all kind of futures

00:55:30.120 --> 00:55:32.640
that we could observe
in our environment,

00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:36.080
we approximate the ground
truth expectation of the values

00:55:36.080 --> 00:55:39.360
by essentially taking a
full episode of interactions

00:55:39.360 --> 00:55:42.800
with the environment until
we reach a terminal condition

00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:44.720
and using the return
that we observe over

00:55:44.720 --> 00:55:46.560
here to update our guess.

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:55.440
Differently, in TD learning,
we defined our update

00:55:55.440 --> 00:55:57.520
as taking a step
in the direction

00:55:57.520 --> 00:56:04.280
of the immediate reward plus
a guess of our future values.

00:56:04.280 --> 00:56:06.160
And so this visually
means that rather

00:56:06.160 --> 00:56:08.760
than having to take a full
episode of interaction

00:56:08.760 --> 00:56:10.920
until reaching a
terminal state, we only

00:56:10.920 --> 00:56:13.980
look at the one step
transition, if that makes sense.

00:56:19.880 --> 00:56:23.590
On the other extreme kind of
positioning this with respect

00:56:23.590 --> 00:56:25.750
to what we saw with
dynamic programming,

00:56:25.750 --> 00:56:27.710
in dynamic programming,
we were essentially

00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:31.710
able to evaluate exactly these
expectations through the use

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:33.590
of the transition dynamics.

00:56:33.590 --> 00:56:35.830
And so we weren't
really defining

00:56:35.830 --> 00:56:38.950
these estimates of these
expectations because we could

00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:45.870
actually compute the exact
expectation by basically

00:56:45.870 --> 00:56:48.507
computing this
expectation analytically

00:56:48.507 --> 00:56:50.090
through the knowledge
of the dynamics.

00:56:59.070 --> 00:57:00.530
Any questions here?

00:57:11.750 --> 00:57:19.410
So we've seen now how
we introduced these two

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:20.550
basic techniques.

00:57:20.550 --> 00:57:26.170
So we discussed sampling as
essentially our mechanism

00:57:26.170 --> 00:57:28.810
to approximate expectations
through interactions

00:57:28.810 --> 00:57:30.170
with the environment.

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:35.090
And we saw how both Monte
Carlo and TD learning sample.

00:57:35.090 --> 00:57:37.190
And this is because they're
model free algorithms.

00:57:37.190 --> 00:57:39.310
So we assume we don't have
knowledge of the dynamics.

00:57:39.310 --> 00:57:40.893
On the other hand,
dynamic programming

00:57:40.893 --> 00:57:42.650
does not sample because
there we assume we

00:57:42.650 --> 00:57:44.510
have knowledge of the dynamics.

00:57:44.510 --> 00:57:48.010
And we could compute those
expectations exactly.

00:57:48.010 --> 00:57:49.690
We then discussed
also bootstrapping

00:57:49.690 --> 00:57:53.290
where the idea is
to basically update

00:57:53.290 --> 00:57:55.950
our guess towards a guess.

00:57:55.950 --> 00:57:59.010
And we saw how both TD learning
and dynamic programming

00:57:59.010 --> 00:58:04.970
were using this idea
while Monte Carlo did not.

00:58:04.970 --> 00:58:07.490
And so, really, what
we're doing today

00:58:07.490 --> 00:58:13.410
is basically
constructing or paving

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:17.080
a unifying view of all
possible reinforcement

00:58:17.080 --> 00:58:20.560
learning algorithms
depending on these two axes.

00:58:20.560 --> 00:58:24.800
And specifically,
thinking about these trees

00:58:24.800 --> 00:58:27.720
that we were observing
before, different algorithms

00:58:27.720 --> 00:58:31.700
fall on a spectrum depending
on the width of the backup.

00:58:31.700 --> 00:58:33.960
So we discussed
algorithms that look

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:36.320
at the full width
of the backup that

00:58:36.320 --> 00:58:39.640
is dynamic programming
or intuitively also

00:58:39.640 --> 00:58:41.080
exhaustive search.

00:58:41.080 --> 00:58:46.440
We could just sample infinitely
until we explore the full tree.

00:58:46.440 --> 00:58:48.680
Or, on the other
hand, algorithms

00:58:48.680 --> 00:58:54.640
that only sample use at
the extreme one sample

00:58:54.640 --> 00:58:55.620
from the environment.

00:58:55.620 --> 00:58:58.120
And so we looked at
temporal difference learning

00:58:58.120 --> 00:59:00.640
and Monte Carlo learning.

00:59:00.640 --> 00:59:03.880
Similarly, we looked at the
height of the backup that

00:59:03.880 --> 00:59:08.880
is through on one extreme Monte
Carlo and exhaustive search

00:59:08.880 --> 00:59:11.800
require reaching the
end, so a terminal state

00:59:11.800 --> 00:59:15.630
in order to update their
values and TD learning

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:20.950
and dynamic programming instead
relied only on a one step update

00:59:20.950 --> 00:59:22.790
because they could
use bootstrapping

00:59:22.790 --> 00:59:26.310
to approximate their values.

00:59:26.310 --> 00:59:29.890
Of course, these are just
four extremes of a spectrum,

00:59:29.890 --> 00:59:32.950
and you could envision
algorithms laying somewhere

00:59:32.950 --> 00:59:33.970
in between, right.

00:59:33.970 --> 00:59:38.270
So, for example, you could
assume rather than updating

00:59:38.270 --> 00:59:41.950
your TD target
after one step, you

00:59:41.950 --> 00:59:44.710
might assume that you want to
do four steps of experience

00:59:44.710 --> 00:59:49.910
and then use the value function
after four steps of experience.

00:59:49.910 --> 00:59:51.930
And there are pros and
cons when doing so.

01:00:05.230 --> 01:00:10.990
OK, so let's now build towards
if you want a first working

01:00:10.990 --> 01:00:14.140
version of a reinforcement
learning algorithm

01:00:14.140 --> 01:00:15.580
using these tools.

01:00:15.580 --> 01:00:18.760
So, in previous lectures, we
introduced policy iteration

01:00:18.760 --> 01:00:20.820
and also earlier today.

01:00:20.820 --> 01:00:23.060
And we essentially
discussed how this

01:00:23.060 --> 01:00:28.360
is an algorithm that implements
two interactive processes.

01:00:28.360 --> 01:00:31.020
We have policy
evaluation on one hand,

01:00:31.020 --> 01:00:34.780
which given a policy estimates
the value for that policy.

01:00:34.780 --> 01:00:36.540
And then we have
policy improvement,

01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:39.420
where given a new estimate
of the value function

01:00:39.420 --> 01:00:42.940
we used it to improve
the current policy.

01:00:42.940 --> 01:00:47.580
In general, we will use the term
generalized policy iteration

01:00:47.580 --> 01:00:51.540
to refer to this general
idea of an algorithm skeleton

01:00:51.540 --> 01:00:55.540
or structure, whereby we will
be iterating between these two

01:00:55.540 --> 01:01:01.540
processes regardless of how
these two processes are actually

01:01:01.540 --> 01:01:03.820
specifically defined.

01:01:03.820 --> 01:01:07.800
In policy iteration, we saw how
the policy evaluation scheme,

01:01:07.800 --> 01:01:12.120
the policy evaluation step
was essentially iterative

01:01:12.120 --> 01:01:16.800
enforcement of the Bellman
expectation equation.

01:01:16.800 --> 01:01:18.280
And for the policy
improvement step

01:01:18.280 --> 01:01:20.800
we used a greedy
policy improvement.

01:01:20.800 --> 01:01:24.640
So we were taking the argmax
over the quantity defined

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:27.000
by the new value function.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:29.180
But this is a general formalism.

01:01:29.180 --> 01:01:31.400
And you will see how
many algorithms actually

01:01:31.400 --> 01:01:33.420
apply this skeleton.

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:42.120
So we mentioned how policy
iteration specifically

01:01:42.120 --> 01:01:44.920
required knowledge
of the dynamics

01:01:44.920 --> 01:01:47.160
in both of these processes.

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:49.160
So we use the knowledge
of the dynamics

01:01:49.160 --> 01:01:52.600
to perform policy evaluation
through the Bellman expectation

01:01:52.600 --> 01:01:53.493
equation.

01:01:53.493 --> 01:01:55.160
And we use the knowledge
of the dynamics

01:01:55.160 --> 01:02:00.120
to improve the policy in
our greedy improvement step.

01:02:00.120 --> 01:02:04.240
So can we make this
actionable through everything

01:02:04.240 --> 01:02:06.160
that we learned today?

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:08.280
So the first thing
that you might think of

01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:13.910
is, can we replace the
analytical policy Bellman

01:02:13.910 --> 01:02:17.070
expectation equation through
Monte Carlo policy evaluation

01:02:17.070 --> 01:02:18.310
of the value function?

01:02:18.310 --> 01:02:20.990
So the first step is
we were given a policy,

01:02:20.990 --> 01:02:23.390
and we want to estimate
the value for that policy.

01:02:23.390 --> 01:02:26.430
Can we do this through Monte
Carlo policy evaluation, which

01:02:26.430 --> 01:02:32.990
is what we've done today and
potentially leave unchanged

01:02:32.990 --> 01:02:35.310
the greedy policy improvement?

01:02:35.310 --> 01:02:38.670
And the answer is that,
well, yes, in the sense

01:02:38.670 --> 01:02:40.872
that Monte Carlo policy
evaluation will work,

01:02:40.872 --> 01:02:42.830
and this is exactly what
we've been doing today

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:46.470
in both in the Monte
Carlo learning section.

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:49.930
But then the greedy policy
improvement, as we said before,

01:02:49.930 --> 01:02:53.550
relied on this knowledge
of the transition dynamics

01:02:53.550 --> 01:02:55.930
to update our policy,
to take this argmax.

01:02:55.930 --> 01:02:59.950
So this will not
cut it in a way.

01:02:59.950 --> 01:03:02.310
So kind of one thing
is that we discussed

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:08.900
before how the Q function is
a nice object in order to--

01:03:08.900 --> 01:03:12.620
and a convenient object in order
to define a policy implicitly.

01:03:12.620 --> 01:03:15.940
So let us just replace
the value function, v,

01:03:15.940 --> 01:03:17.120
with the Q function.

01:03:22.180 --> 01:03:25.340
So now we're looking at a
version of policy iteration

01:03:25.340 --> 01:03:29.620
where we are doing Monte Carlo
policy evaluation of the Q

01:03:29.620 --> 01:03:33.420
function and then potentially
greedy improvement.

01:03:33.420 --> 01:03:37.220
So will this work?

01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:38.420
What do you think?

01:03:38.420 --> 01:03:41.380
Well, there is a spoiler
on the slides, of course.

01:03:41.380 --> 01:03:43.040
What do you think and why?

01:03:51.060 --> 01:03:55.520
Well, the point is that
it won't, in general.

01:03:55.520 --> 01:03:59.260
And the idea is that here it
kind of touches on, I would say,

01:03:59.260 --> 01:04:02.220
a really fundamental
issue and challenge

01:04:02.220 --> 01:04:05.800
in reinforcement learning,
which is one of exploration.

01:04:05.800 --> 01:04:09.770
So the trade off between
exploitation and exploration.

01:04:09.770 --> 01:04:15.810
So let us look at this from the
perspective of a simple example.

01:04:15.810 --> 01:04:19.490
So say we have two
doors and we need

01:04:19.490 --> 01:04:22.490
to pick among these two doors
and each of these two doors

01:04:22.490 --> 01:04:25.170
will give us a specific reward.

01:04:25.170 --> 01:04:27.490
This is in a way
stateless environment

01:04:27.490 --> 01:04:29.770
because we are always
in the same state.

01:04:29.770 --> 01:04:31.450
So we have nothing
that conditions us

01:04:31.450 --> 01:04:33.610
in picking the two doors.

01:04:33.610 --> 01:04:35.490
But now say that we
have no information

01:04:35.490 --> 01:04:39.530
of the quality of the two doors
and that we, first of all,

01:04:39.530 --> 01:04:44.350
so we initialize our Q function
randomly and then also randomly,

01:04:44.350 --> 01:04:48.650
we basically pick the left
door as our first interaction,

01:04:48.650 --> 01:04:50.730
and we get a reward of 0.

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:56.810
So we basically update the
value of our Q function as 0.

01:04:56.810 --> 01:05:01.510
Now, we similarly execute
in our environment,

01:05:01.510 --> 01:05:04.170
and we pick the right door
and we observe a reward of 1

01:05:04.170 --> 01:05:04.790
in this case.

01:05:04.790 --> 01:05:09.710
So we update the value of the
Q. Now, note that in general,

01:05:09.710 --> 01:05:12.790
right now, the policy that we
are executing in the environment

01:05:12.790 --> 01:05:14.510
is a deterministic policy.

01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:17.750
It is the argmax
over the Q function.

01:05:17.750 --> 01:05:21.070
So since we have basically
created a situation

01:05:21.070 --> 01:05:24.950
where the right
choice is basically

01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:29.290
dominating the left door,
here and basically forever

01:05:29.290 --> 01:05:32.590
we will always be
choosing the right door.

01:05:32.590 --> 01:05:37.950
And so this is potentially
at loss of a better reward

01:05:37.950 --> 01:05:40.050
that we might have
had on the left door.

01:05:42.630 --> 01:05:48.070
And so the problem here is once
we estimate Q functions or state

01:05:48.070 --> 01:05:51.070
action values
through samples, then

01:05:51.070 --> 01:05:53.870
we need to visit every
state action pair in order

01:05:53.870 --> 01:05:56.370
to approximate those values.

01:05:59.150 --> 01:06:03.460
And deterministic policies do
not allow for this exploration.

01:06:03.460 --> 01:06:06.780
So a simple but effective
trick and strategy

01:06:06.780 --> 01:06:10.260
is to basically
introduce some randomness

01:06:10.260 --> 01:06:12.060
in how we explore
the environment

01:06:12.060 --> 01:06:13.620
and how we take decisions.

01:06:13.620 --> 01:06:17.700
And a simple strategy is one
of epsilon-greedy exploration.

01:06:17.700 --> 01:06:23.540
Specifically, with some
probability epsilon,

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:26.700
1 minus epsilon, we will
be consistent with how

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:28.640
we pick the action so far.

01:06:28.640 --> 01:06:31.340
So we will just take
the argmax over Q.

01:06:31.340 --> 01:06:34.420
On the other hand, with some
small probability epsilon,

01:06:34.420 --> 01:06:36.520
we will be acting random.

01:06:36.520 --> 01:06:38.140
So we'll just pick
a random actions

01:06:38.140 --> 01:06:42.300
from our possible action space.

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:45.900
This is a very simple trick
but fundamentally, this

01:06:45.900 --> 01:06:50.380
ensures that all actions are
tried with non-zero probability.

01:06:50.380 --> 01:06:52.660
And so this, on the long
run, ensures that we

01:06:52.660 --> 01:06:54.780
are visiting and exploring.

01:06:54.780 --> 01:06:58.460
And that is having a nice
coverage of our entire state

01:06:58.460 --> 01:06:59.320
action space.

01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:08.410
And so if you wish this
builds and leads us

01:07:08.410 --> 01:07:12.050
to a first working version
of a reinforcement learning

01:07:12.050 --> 01:07:13.647
algorithm.

01:07:13.647 --> 01:07:15.730
That is something where
we have Monte Carlo policy

01:07:15.730 --> 01:07:19.450
evaluation of a Q function and
then an epsilon greedy policy

01:07:19.450 --> 01:07:20.230
improvement.

01:07:24.010 --> 01:07:28.610
So since the goal for today is
becoming experts at blackjack,

01:07:28.610 --> 01:07:31.250
let's look at how the
policy that we would

01:07:31.250 --> 01:07:34.410
get if we run this in practice
on the same environment

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:35.690
that we had before.

01:07:35.690 --> 01:07:39.370
So now before we were
focusing on policy evaluation.

01:07:39.370 --> 01:07:41.570
So we were given
a specific policy

01:07:41.570 --> 01:07:48.250
that was standing if the sum
of cards was 20 or above.

01:07:48.250 --> 01:07:50.730
Now, we're actually
doing the full problem

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:56.330
where we actually want to derive
an optimal policy for the task.

01:07:56.330 --> 01:07:59.920
And so here if you trust
me, that by running exactly

01:07:59.920 --> 01:08:02.720
the algorithm that
we discussed so far,

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:07.400
we would basically be converging
to this policy over here,

01:08:07.400 --> 01:08:12.960
whereby we have a specific rule
that we can also inspect where

01:08:12.960 --> 01:08:16.000
depending on if we have
a usable ace or not,

01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:19.859
and the sum of the cards and
the dealer's showing card,

01:08:19.859 --> 01:08:23.359
we have an explicit rule of
whether to stick or stand

01:08:23.359 --> 01:08:24.380
or hit.

01:08:27.720 --> 01:08:32.740
A note maybe intuitively that
if we don't have a usable ace,

01:08:32.740 --> 01:08:35.319
we're actually more
conservative here.

01:08:35.319 --> 01:08:37.300
So, of course, we can
go into the details.

01:08:37.300 --> 01:08:40.279
And I'm definitely not advising
you to try this strategy if you

01:08:40.279 --> 01:08:41.939
go in because who knows.

01:08:41.939 --> 01:08:43.399
But this is the idea.

01:08:43.399 --> 01:08:46.840
Again, we gave no
clue, no intuition

01:08:46.840 --> 01:08:49.240
whatsoever to the
environment on how to behave.

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:53.800
And through this interaction, we
derived both an optimal policy

01:08:53.800 --> 01:08:56.300
and its respective
value function.

01:09:02.899 --> 01:09:07.460
OK, so to recap what we've
been discussing so far,

01:09:07.460 --> 01:09:12.660
so we started from exact methods
for solving Markov decision

01:09:12.660 --> 01:09:17.500
processes, namely policy
iteration and value iteration.

01:09:17.500 --> 01:09:21.060
And we said and recognized that
these have some limitations.

01:09:21.060 --> 01:09:24.180
Specifically, today we looked
at the first of these two,

01:09:24.180 --> 01:09:29.819
which is the one that in
both the update equations

01:09:29.819 --> 01:09:34.540
and of our estimates for
the value function, that

01:09:34.540 --> 01:09:36.580
is the Bellman equations.

01:09:36.580 --> 01:09:40.180
And in the greedy policy
improvement steps,

01:09:40.180 --> 01:09:42.859
we were using
implicitly our knowledge

01:09:42.859 --> 01:09:44.520
of the transition dynamics.

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:47.340
And so, today, we focus
on these two main classes

01:09:47.340 --> 01:09:50.180
of methods and families
of methods, MC learning

01:09:50.180 --> 01:09:53.359
and TD learning to address
this through interaction.

01:09:57.410 --> 01:10:00.930
And so, indeed, so we introduced
MC learning and TD learning

01:10:00.930 --> 01:10:06.170
as examples of a spectrum,
where depending on the height

01:10:06.170 --> 01:10:11.170
or width of the backup, we could
derive different algorithms

01:10:11.170 --> 01:10:14.850
all under the kind of
shared goal of solving

01:10:14.850 --> 01:10:18.370
unknown MDPs through learning.

01:10:18.370 --> 01:10:21.690
We did not discuss how we could
deal with high dimensional state

01:10:21.690 --> 01:10:27.370
action spaces, but we'll do
this in the coming lectures.

01:10:27.370 --> 01:10:30.690
Any questions here before
I spend a few minutes

01:10:30.690 --> 01:10:32.510
on some concluding remarks?

01:10:40.810 --> 01:10:42.570
So if you recall,
last week we were

01:10:42.570 --> 01:10:44.730
looking at this taxonomy
of reinforcement learning

01:10:44.730 --> 01:10:45.430
algorithms.

01:10:48.370 --> 01:10:52.090
Starting next week, we'll
be looking at, in order,

01:10:52.090 --> 01:10:55.360
we'll be looking at value
based methods next Monday.

01:10:55.360 --> 01:10:58.800
We'll then be looking at policy
optimization and next Wednesday.

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:02.440
And then we'll be looking
at model based algorithms.

01:11:02.440 --> 01:11:06.380
Well, not next Monday because
that's a holiday but next time.

01:11:06.380 --> 01:11:10.360
And then we'll be looking
at model based algorithms.

01:11:10.360 --> 01:11:16.240
In general, I think
it is really valuable

01:11:16.240 --> 01:11:20.840
to see all of these algorithms
as different instantiations

01:11:20.840 --> 01:11:23.800
of a common, shared skeleton
of our reinforcement

01:11:23.800 --> 01:11:25.240
learning algorithm.

01:11:25.240 --> 01:11:28.800
And specifically, this skeleton
has three main processes

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:30.040
going on.

01:11:30.040 --> 01:11:32.480
The first one is
that all algorithms

01:11:32.480 --> 01:11:35.280
will have some way
of generating samples

01:11:35.280 --> 01:11:37.500
because we want to
learn from interaction.

01:11:37.500 --> 01:11:39.360
And as we saw today,
interaction for us

01:11:39.360 --> 01:11:43.040
essentially means
sampling episodes

01:11:43.040 --> 01:11:47.320
from interactions with the
environment, where the goal here

01:11:47.320 --> 01:11:50.440
will be essentially to
collect these trajectories

01:11:50.440 --> 01:11:53.510
or episodes of
interactions, tau,

01:11:53.510 --> 01:11:57.750
and use these quantities to
basically do something with it.

01:11:57.750 --> 01:12:00.790
Specifically, we'll
use these samples

01:12:00.790 --> 01:12:03.510
from the environment
to either fit a model

01:12:03.510 --> 01:12:06.190
or estimate a return or a value.

01:12:06.190 --> 01:12:08.190
So today we
discussed extensively

01:12:08.190 --> 01:12:12.470
how we might be interested in
estimating value functions or Q

01:12:12.470 --> 01:12:14.310
functions.

01:12:14.310 --> 01:12:15.830
But, in general,
we might also see

01:12:15.830 --> 01:12:19.910
how we could use these
trajectories to estimate

01:12:19.910 --> 01:12:23.190
a learned model of our
dynamics in model free RL.

01:12:23.190 --> 01:12:25.110
Bottom line is we will
have some interactions

01:12:25.110 --> 01:12:27.270
with our environment, and
we'll be doing something

01:12:27.270 --> 01:12:29.030
with that interaction,
which is, loosely

01:12:29.030 --> 01:12:31.150
speaking either
estimating some values

01:12:31.150 --> 01:12:36.350
or fitting some model
of our dynamics.

01:12:36.350 --> 01:12:39.030
And then we'll use
these computations

01:12:39.030 --> 01:12:42.390
that we do in the second
block to improve the policy.

01:12:42.390 --> 01:12:44.190
Today, we saw how
we are basically

01:12:44.190 --> 01:12:48.210
using an epsilon greedy or
greedy policy improvement step.

01:12:48.210 --> 01:12:53.690
But we will see different
ways of improving the policy.

01:12:53.690 --> 01:12:57.650
For example, we'll see how in
policy optimization algorithms

01:12:57.650 --> 01:13:02.370
we will be taking gradient
steps with respect

01:13:02.370 --> 01:13:05.410
to some parameters, theta,
which might represent

01:13:05.410 --> 01:13:09.450
a parametric policy that
we're defining in order

01:13:09.450 --> 01:13:12.030
to optimize the RL
objective directly,

01:13:12.030 --> 01:13:14.890
that is maximized
estimated return.

01:13:14.890 --> 01:13:16.950
But we'll see this
more next week.

01:13:20.210 --> 01:13:24.650
But bottom line is I think this
is a very useful mental model

01:13:24.650 --> 01:13:27.090
to use when approaching
different RL

01:13:27.090 --> 01:13:28.850
algorithms because
this is really

01:13:28.850 --> 01:13:31.550
a kind of other core of what
most of these are doing.

01:13:37.890 --> 01:13:40.130
So if there are
no more questions,

01:13:40.130 --> 01:13:43.050
I'll see you next time with
value-based reinforcement

01:13:43.050 --> 01:13:44.210
learning.

01:13:44.210 --> 01:13:46.040
Thank you.

